  "blisterPreventionHeader": "Preventing blisters",
  "@blisterPreventionHeader": {
    "description": "prevention for blisters header"
  },
  "blisterPrevention": "● Wear shoes and socks that fit 👟🥾 not too big, not too tight\n● Always wear socks with your shoes 🧦\n● Keep feet dry as moist feet are more prone to blisters",
  "@blisterPrevention": {
    "description": "prevention for blisters"
  },
  "blisterPreventionWarning": "Always seek medical help if you notice signs of infection or have other medical issues like diabetes or circulation problems",
  "@blisterPreventionWarning": {
    "description": "prevention warning for blisters"
  },
  "blisterSxHeader": "Blister symptoms",
  "@blisterSxHeader": {
    "description": "blisters sx header"
  },
  "blisterSx": "● Round fluid-filled vesicles (fluid is typically clear)\n● Redness around the area 🔴\n● Painful burning sensation 🔥",
  "@blisterSx": {
    "description": "blisters sx"
  },
  "blisterTxHeader": "Treatment for blisters",
  "@blisterTxHeader": {
    "description": "tx for blisters header"
  },
  "blisterTx": "● Never break or pop the blister 🎈💥 This increases the risk of infection\n● Use bandages, protective dressings or clean socks to add padding to protect the blister\n● There are special bandages that can help, called hydrocolloidal dressings — ask the pharmacist for help\n● If the blister pops, clean it with warm water and mild soap for 5-10 minutes\n● Clean the blister every day and watch for signs of infection",
  "@blisterTx": {
    "description": "tx for blisters"
  },
  "burns": "Burns",
  "@burns": {
    "description": "header title for burns"
  },
  "burnsDef": "Burns can occur due to exposure to heat, like hot liquids, steam, fire, or hot objects like a stove",
  "@burnsDef": {
    "description": "def for burns"
  },
  "burnsLabel": "Skin was burned by a heat source",
  "@burnsLabel": {
    "description": "label for burns"
  },
  "burnsFollowupHeader": "How to care for a burn",
  "@burnsFollowupHeader": {
    "description": "follow—ups for burns header"
  },
  "burnsFollowup": "\n● Wash the burn with soap and water every day and apply a new dressing\n● Monitor the burn for signs of infection\n● If blisters do develop, never pop them\n● If the burn begins to itch, do not scratch it\n● Superficial burns can take up to a week to heal; deeper burns will take longer to heal",
  "@burnsFollowup": {
    "description": "follow—ups for burns"
  },
  "burnsImages": "Click here to see images of burns",
  "@burnsImages": {
    "description": "images of burns"
  },
  "burnsFirstDegree1": "1st degree: Skin is red and dry, only the top layer of the skin is affected",
  "@burnsFirstDegree1": {
    "description": "image of burns"
  },
  "burnsFirstDegree2": "1st degree: Skin has a darker color and is dry; note the arm hair that has been burned off",
  "@burnsFirstDegree2": {
    "description": "image of burns"
  },
  "burnsFirstDegree3": "1st degree: Skin has a darker color and is dry; a small blister is forming",
  "@burnsFirstDegree3": {
    "description": "image of burns"
  },
  "burnsSecondDegree1": "2nd degree: Skin is very red and swollen; the very top layer of the skin is peeling off ",
  "@burnsSecondDegree1": {
    "description": "image of burns"
  },
  "burnsSecondDegree2": "2nd degree: Skin is slightly discolored, seems mildly swollen; fluid-filled blisters have appeared",
  "@burnsSecondDegree2": {
    "description": "image of burns"
  },
  "burnsSecondDegree3": "2nd degree: Another example of blisters that can occur; note the darker skin around the blisters",
  "@burnsSecondDegree3": {
    "description": "image of burns"
  },
  "burnsSecondDegree4": "2nd degree: Underlying skin is red and seeping clear fluid (has a wet look)",
  "@burnsSecondDegree4": {
    "description": "image of burns"
  },
  "burnsThirdDegree1": "3rd degree: Complete loss of dark skin tone, burn appears white; lots of redness and skin is peeling",
  "@burnsThirdDegree1": {
    "description": "image of burns"
  },
  "burnsThirdDegree2": "3rd degree: Multiple layers of skin are visible",
  "@burnsThirdDegree2": {
    "description": "image of burns"
  },
  "burnsThirdDegree3": "3rd degree: Deeper wound with a dry, crusted, darker center and 2nd degree burns around it",
  "@burnsThirdDegree3": {
    "description": "image of burns"
  },
  "burnsSxHeader": "Symptoms depend on how intense the burn is",
  "@burnsSxHeader": {
    "description": "burns sx"
  },
  "burnsSx": "● For superficial burns (1st degree) the skin is red, dry and painful 🔴\n● Partial thickness burns (2nd degree) are deeper. Skin appears red, discolored and will have blisters 💢\n● Full thickness burns (3rd degree) appear white, gray or black and you won't feel pain",
  "@burnsSx": {
    "description": "burns sx"
  },
  "burnsTxHeader": "Immediate treatment for burns",
  "@burnsTxHeader": {
    "description": "burns tx header"
  },
  "burnsTxStep1": "● Immediately remove any clothing that is covering the burn. If the clothes stick to the skin, immediately go to an emergency room, do not try to remove them yourself",
  "@burnsTxStep1": {
    "description": "tx for burns step 1"
  },
  "burnsTxStep2": "● Cool the area by running cold water on the burn continuously for at least 15 minutes\n● Never put ice on the burn 🧊❌",
  "@burnsTxStep2": {
    "description": "tx for burns step 2"
  },
  "burnsTxStep3": "● After cooling the burn for 15 minutes, wash the area with cool water and a mild soap. Let air dry or very gently pat dry with a clean cloth",
  "@burnsTxStep3": {
    "description": "tx for burns step 3"
  },
  "burnsTxStep4": "● Do not apply any creams or products to the burn 🧴❌\n● Apply a non-stick dressing to the burn",
  "@burnsTxStep4": {
    "description": "tx for burns step 4"
  },
  "burnsWarning": "Seek help for any burn that:\n● Is a 2nd or 3rd degree burn\n● Involves the face, hands, fingers, genitals or feet\n● Is larger than the size of an orange 🍊\n● If there are signs of infection",
  "@burnsWarning": {
    "description": "prevention warning for burns"
  },
  "callus": "Corns and calluses",
  "@callus": {
    "description": "header for callus"
  },
  "callusDef": "Calluses are large patches of thickened skin. Corns are typically small, localized areas of hard skin that can be painful. Both occur because of repeated rubbing on the foot (for example, by wearing ill-fitting shoes)",
  "@callusDef": {
    "description": "def for header"
  },
  "callusLabel": "Thickened skin on the foot",
  "@callusLabel": {
    "description": "def for header"
  },
  "callusImages": "Click here to see images of corns and calluses",
  "@callusImages": {
    "description": "images of calluses"
  },
  "callusImage1": "Calluses. Thickened, yellowed skin and some peeling on the soles of the feet",
  "@callusImage1": {
    "description": "images of corns & calluses"
  },
  "callusImage2": "Calluses. Thickened skin with a yellowish color on the side of the foot",
  "@callusImage2": {
    "description": "images of corns & calluses"
  },
  "callusImage3": "Calluses. Thickened skin with small fissures on the heels of the feet",
  "@callusImage3": {
    "description": "images of corns & calluses"
  },
  "callusImage4": "Corns. Hard and dark bump on the toe",
  "@callusImage4": {
    "description": "images of corns & calluses"
  },
  "callusImage5": "Corns. Hard bump with peeling skin and redness on the toe",
  "@callusImage5": {
    "description": "images of corns & calluses"
  },
  "callusImage6": "Corns. Hard bump with thickened skin; visible peeling and redness around the corn",
  "@callusImage6": {
    "description": "images of corns & calluses"
  },
  "callusPreventionHeader": "Preventing corns and calluses",
  "@callusPreventionHeader": {
    "description": "prevention for calluses header"
  },
  "callusPrevention": "● Wear shoes and socks that fit — not too big, not too tight 👟🥾 Your toes should be able to move freely\n● Always wear socks with your shoes 🧦\n● Avoid walking barefoot\n● Keep your feet clean and dry",
  "@callusPrevention": {
    "description": "prevention for calluses"
  },
  "callusPreventionWarning": "Always seek medical help if you notice signs of infection or have other medical issues like diabetes or circulation problems",
  "@callusPreventionWarning": {
    "description": "prevention warning for calluses"
  },
  "callusSxHeader": "Calluses and corns symptoms",
  "@callusSxHeader": {
    "description": "sx for calluses header"
  },
  "callusSx": "● Calluses: hardening and thickening of the skin; it might peel or crack\n● Corns: hard bumps form on pressure points of the foot",
  "@callusSx": {
    "description": "sx for calluses"
  },
  "callusTxHeader": "Treatment for calluses and corns",
  "@callusTxHeader": {
    "description": "tx for calluses header"
  },
  "callusTx": "● Never attempt to remove a callus or corn by yourself\n● Apply a thin layer of hydrating cream on your feet, especially on the heel 🧴\n● Avoid putting too much cream between the toes, this area should stay dry\n● Sometimes, soaking your feet in warm water for 20 minutes can help soften calluses 🦶🛁\n● There are plasters that you can buy at the pharmacy that can help — ask the pharmacist for help",
  "@callusTx": {
    "description": "tx for calluses"
  },
